cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite it and the paper it accompanies once published. This artifact accompanies a prototype-stage manuscript that is not yet submitted (see README.md); cite with that context."
title: "DAIM-OS Self-Healing Artifact: Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Banjar
    given-names: Ameen
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0871-5153"
version: 0.7.0
date-released: 2026-08-13
license: Apache-2.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21924978
# v0.6.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21918394. v0.5.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21912414.
# v0.4.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21910227. v0.3.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21909633.
# v0.1.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21906993.
# Concept DOI (always resolves to latest version): 10.5281/zenodo.21906992.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ameen-banjar/daim-os-self-healing-artifact"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility artifact for "Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a
  DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
  (Paper 3 in the DAIM six-paper programme, prototype-stage, not yet
  submitted). Contains an event-driven repair agent (daim_link_agent.py)
  that subscribes to real OVSDB Interface.link_state notifications,
  recomputes an alternate path with breadth-first search over a declared
  topology graph, installs it through the existing DAIM-OS OVS adapter, and
  suppresses repeated repair processing on a flapping link via an
  IDLE/ACTIVE/HELD-DOWN hold-down state machine, keyed by physical edge
  rather than interface name since v0.3.0. An edge is only treated as
  recovered once every interface observing it independently confirms `up`
  (v0.4.0) -- a persistent per-interface state, not a single last-report-wins
  value per edge. A fresh agent process also correctly reads OVSDB's initial
  table snapshot at startup (v0.5.0), rather than only matching later
  "new"-actioned rows, so an interface already down when the agent starts is
  detected and routed around instead of silently assumed up; that snapshot
  is now also validated to be exactly "up" or "down" rather than treating
  any unrecognised value as implicitly up, and every fatal startup path
  cleans up the OVSDB monitor subprocess rather than leaking it (v0.6.0).
  Four real defects are documented in the history: a stale-state bug
  (v0.2.0, found via external manuscript review); a cross-interface
  hold-down gap (v0.3.0, found by the first live-network run of the
  flapping-link protocol against a real Mininet/OVS link -- OVSDB reports a
  link's two interfaces' transitions independently, and the pre-fix
  hold-down only covered the interface that triggered the repair); a
  last-report-wins edge-recovery gap (v0.4.0, found by code review); and a
  startup-state synchronization gap (v0.5.0, found by code review and
  confirmed against a live ovsdb-server -- the pre-fix agent produced 100%
  packet loss when a link was already down at startup, 0% after the fix). A
  fifth, separate bug in this artifact's own verification harness
  (stage3_startup_already_down.py's packet-loss check, a substring match
  that misclassified a 100%-loss run as loss-free) is also documented and
  fixed (v0.6.0), with its own regression test -- the underlying 100%/0%
  result was unaffected, since the figure-generation script parsed the
  numeric loss value independently, but the harness's own boolean field was
  wrong until this fix. v0.7.0 extends link_state validation to the ongoing
  event stream, not just the startup snapshot (decide_link_event() now
  returns a distinct invalid_link_state action instead of silently falling
  through to a no-op), and fixes a mislabelling bug in the flapping-link
  comparison figure: its "BFS/recompute calls" row previously counted every
  non-suppressed action including "recovered", which never calls BFS, so it
  reported 4-to-2 when the real count (repair/noop/repair_failed only) is
  1-to-1 for this specific live schedule -- the BFS-call-reduction claim is
  Figure 5's logic-level result, not this live figure's. Contains pure-logic
  unit tests for the path computation and all hold-down/startup behaviours
  (suppression, stale-state reconciliation, cross-interface coverage,
  edge-confirmation, startup synchronization, link_state validation at both
  startup and runtime), all
  runnable without Mininet/OVS; live-network flapping-link and
  startup-already-down scripts and their raw data and event logs across every
  pre-fix and post-fix run, retained side by side; the network-level Stage 3
  raw data (five autonomous link-failure repetitions, plus scripted
  non-autonomous switch/controller-failure conditions) and reports; and the
  figure-generation script and resulting figures used in the manuscript.
keywords:
  - software-defined networking
  - self-healing networks
  - event-driven fault detection
  - autonomous path recovery
  - OVSDB
  - OpenFlow
  - hold-down timers
  - control-plane resilience
  - reproducible systems research
